  [1mB — Explicit [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter, threaded.[0m Literally the original ask: [38;5;153mlogin(userId, username, password)[39m, and every
  future form function takes [38;5;153muserId[39m too. Since no id exists before authentication, it would have to be a
  client-generated pseudonymous device id kept in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m explicit, no hidden state, matches what you asked for. [3mCosts:[0m prop-drilling through every form, and it conflates
  two different things — a forgeable device id and the real authenticated user. [3mRules out:[0m little technically, but a
  client-minted id is weak evidence in a compliance trail, which is the one thing you said this is for.

  [1mC — Audited request chokepoint.[0m One [38;5;153mapiRequest()[39m wrapper every form uses. It attaches the correlation id and current
  identity and shapes the audit envelope in a single place. Forms never handle [38;5;153muserId[39m themselves.
  [3mBuys:[0m a form physically cannot forget to be audited. [3mCosts:[0m an abstraction layer and a backend contract pinned down up
  front. [3mRules out:[0m ad-hoc direct [38;5;153mfetch[39m calls, deliberately.

  [1mMy recommendation: C[0m, which absorbs A's context module as its internals. "Other forms will need it later" plus
  compliance means the failure mode that matters is a future form silently not being audited — a chokepoint is the only
  one of the three that makes that impossible rather than merely discouraged. Its extra cost is small here precisely
  because the repo is nearly empty. I'd steer away from B: it's the literal request, but it would put a value the client
  invented into a record meant to prove who accessed what.

  Separately — this repo has no tooling at all ([38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies and no [38;5;153mscripts[39m; no test runner, linter,
  or formatter). Tooling is cheapest to add before the code exists, and for compliance code the tests are part of the
  argument that it works.
